    "done_when": "Read the plan section with read_plan(view='section', section='s4') first; it is the authority. Today recovery.classify_pointer costs about 8.4 s over 12 pointers in a cold obligations call, and _backends.observe_log and parse_events re-parse whole worker streams (about 4.2 s). After the change, the memo is persisted in a file beside each pointer, under the live pointer directory, and its key covers every file the classification reads. The pointer, manifest, stream and review record are the files known today; establish the full set by reading the code, and list it in the manifest. Stream observation resumes from a byte offset persisted in the same memo file. A memo whose key no longer matches is recomputed, never served. Record the base arm first: time obligations('reckon', 's21-1c') in a fresh process at base and at head, into a log under the run directory, as measurement only. In a new tests/test_pointer_classification_is_memoised.py, built on synthesised pointers, manifests and streams under the test's temporary path and a temporary configuration home: a second classification with no input changed does not call parse_events (patched to count); appending to one pointer's stream re-observes only the appended bytes, and only that pointer is reclassified; changing any one input file (manifest, review record, pointer) reclassifies that pointer; and the memoised and uncached classifications agree for every pointer (the parity case). The named negative control (the memo key without the review record) is executed and fails the review-record-changed case. Keep the change inside classify_pointer, its stream observation and the memo; another live node edits obligations._held_worktrees. The gate is the new test plus every existing test file under tests/ that calls classify_pointer, observe_log or observe_stream, run at base and at head with -n 8 and --timeout=300; list them in the manifest. Each arm is recorded as JSON carrying completed true and a literal command with absolute paths and no angle-bracket placeholder. Each log names its revision on the first line and ends with a bare line reading EXIT= followed by the exit code. Every pytest process uses a basetemp private to that process. The head arm adds no failure id; any added id means status blocked, naming each id. ruff check and ruff format --check over the changed Python paths report no finding the base did not. Scratch goes under /tmp in a directory named for this run and is deleted before status complete. An evidence fragment at docs/evidence/fragments/the-obligations-hook-reads-a-snapshot/pointer-classification-is-memoised.html records the change and both timings. The turn ends once the manifest reads complete or blocked.",
